      <p><strong>Aerosol dip tube design</strong> determines how completely a can empties and how steady its spray stays from the first actuation to the last. The dip tube is the plastic tube that runs from the valve to the bottom of the can, carrying liquid product up to the valve under propellant pressure. Its length, bore, material and end cut control residual product, spray rate and compatibility with the formulation, which is why it deserves deliberate specification and not a catalogue default. This guide explains how a dip tube works, how length and positioning decide product depletion, how bore size and the vapor tap shape spray consistency, how to match tube material to the formula, and how to test a design before scale-up. It extends the pressure and valve fundamentals in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/how-aerosol-cans-work/" target="_top">How Aerosol Cans Work guide</a> and sits within the wider <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-technology-and-formulations/" target="_top">aerosol technology and formulations</a> knowledge base.</p>

      <section id="section-1">
        <h2>How an Aerosol Dip Tube Works</h2>
        <p>In a standard upright aerosol, the valve sits at the top of the can while the liquid product collects at the bottom, so something has to bridge the gap. The dip tube does that job. When the actuator opens the valve, propellant pressure pushes the liquid up the tube, through the valve body and out of the orifices as a spray. Because the tube is the first restriction in the flow path, its design shapes everything that happens downstream of it.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Dip tube:</strong> carries liquid from the base of the can to the valve; its length sets where liquid is drawn from and its bore sets the first flow resistance</li>
          <li><strong>Valve body and stem orifices:</strong> meter the flow and begin atomisation as the product leaves the valve</li>
          <li><strong>Actuator:</strong> shapes the final spray pattern once the product exits the stem</li>
          <li><strong>Propellant:</strong> supplies the pressure that drives liquid up the tube</li>
        </ul>
        <p>These parts act as a series of restrictions, so the dip tube cannot be specified in isolation. Reducing the bore of the dip tube, like reducing the body or stem orifice, generally lowers the spray rate and the particle size and can increase the amount of product retained in the can. The tube also connects to the valve body, and the elastomer gasket materials that govern long-term sealing against the formulation are covered in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-valve-gasket-explained/" target="_top">aerosol valve gasket materials guide</a>.</p>
        <p>Not every aerosol uses a dip tube. Bag-on-valve systems hold the product in a sealed, laminated pouch separated from the propellant, which removes the dip tube and allows dispensing in any orientation. That design is explored in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/bag-on-valve-technology-design-and-applications/" target="_top">bag-on-valve technology guide</a> and summarised in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aerosol_spray" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikipedia aerosol spray overview</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="section-2">
        <h2>Dip Tube Length and Positioning: Controlling Product Depletion</h2>
        <p>Dip tube length decides how much liquid is left in the can when spraying stops. A tube that reaches close to the base of the can draws product down to a small residue, while a tube that stops short leaves liquid beneath its inlet that the user can never spray. Length therefore has to be matched to the internal height of the container and the installed height of the valve assembly, because tube, valve and can all vary slightly from part to part. A correctly sized tube allows containers to be substantially emptied despite that manufacturing variation.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Tube condition</th><th>Effect on depletion</th><th>Effect on spray</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Too short</td><td>Liquid remains beneath the tube inlet, so residual product is higher</td><td>Spray can turn gassy while product is still in the can</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Matched to container height</td><td>The inlet reaches the lowest liquid, so residue is minimal</td><td>Liquid is drawn consistently until the can is nearly empty</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Too long</td><td>The tip can seat flat against the can base and restrict the inlet</td><td>The draw can be reduced or interrupted</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>End geometry matters as much as length. Dip tubes are commonly supplied with a straight or an angled cut, and an angled end keeps an entry path for liquid open if the tip meets the base of the can. Tube stiffness also affects positioning, because a flexible tube can curl as it is inserted through the can while a stiffer tube holds a straighter line to the base. Assembly faults of this kind sit alongside the valve and seal failures catalogued in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/why-do-aerosol-cans-leak/" target="_top">why aerosol cans leak guide</a>.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-amber">
          <strong>Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Specify dip tube length from measured, filled-can geometry rather than a catalogue default: take the installed valve height and the internal can depth from crimped production cans, then confirm the length with a depletion test.
        </div>
      </section>

