      <section id="section-3">
        <h2>Bore Size, Vapor Taps and Aerosol Spray Consistency</h2>
        <p>Spray consistency means the last sprays from a can look and feel like the first. Three dip tube features influence it: the internal bore of the tube, the viscosity of the product it has to carry, and the presence of a vapor tap. The bore sets the flow resistance of the first stage of the flow path, so it has to be balanced against the body and stem orifices and not chosen alone.</p>
        <p>A vapor tap is a small hole in the dip tube or valve body that lets propellant vapor mix with the liquid on its way to the valve. In the valve patent literature the hole sits in the tube above the liquid entrance and below the initial liquid level, so that once the liquid level falls below it, vapor enters the tube and mixes with the remaining liquid, substantially re-establishing the spray characteristics that would otherwise deteriorate late in the can's life. The hole is smaller than both the exit orifice and the bore of the tube, and the ratio of these cross-sectional areas is critical to how well it works.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Design feature</th><th>What it changes</th><th>Design consideration</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Dip tube bore</td><td>Sets first-stage flow resistance; a smaller bore generally lowers spray rate and particle size and can increase retained product</td><td>Balance against the body and stem orifices; never size it alone</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Product viscosity</td><td>A more viscous product meets higher resistance in a narrow tube</td><td>The flow path must not choke the draw of a thick formulation</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Vapor tap hole</td><td>Lets propellant vapor mix with liquid, restoring spray characteristics once the level falls below the hole</td><td>Smaller than the exit orifice and the tube bore; the area ratio is critical</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Valve architecture</td><td>Changes the downstream path the liquid takes after the tube</td><td>Review alongside <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/tilt-valve-vs-vertical-valve-aerosol-selection/" target="_top">tilt valve versus vertical valve selection</a></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>The effect of bore on flow follows the same fluid mechanics that govern flow through any narrow tube: resistance rises as the bore narrows and as the fluid becomes more viscous, as described by the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hagen%E2%80%93Poiseuille_equation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hagen–Poiseuille equation</a>. For a dip tube this means a thick formulation and a narrow bore compound each other, and the combination shows up as a weak or uneven spray.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key Insight</strong>
          Spray inconsistency late in a can's life can originate in the dip tube and valve path as well as in the formulation, so check tube length, bore and vapor tap before reformulating.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="section-4">
        <h2>Dip Tube Material and Chemical Compatibility</h2>
        <p>Dip tubes are commonly extruded from <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Polyethylene" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">polyethylene</a> in LDPE, MDPE or HDPE grades, or from <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Polypropylene" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">polypropylene</a>, and the choice is driven by compatibility with the formulation. The tube sits in constant contact with solvents, actives and liquefied propellant for the full shelf life, so a mismatch shows up as swelling, softening, stiffening or cracking. Patent literature on dip tubes notes that environmental-stress-cracking-resistant grades of polypropylene or HDPE matter where the product is prone to causing that failure, and that the resistance is unnecessary where no such substance is present.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Material</th><th>Character</th><th>Compatibility consideration</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Polyethylene (LDPE, MDPE, HDPE)</td><td>Flexible and widely extruded for dip tubes; stiffness rises from low- to high-density grades</td><td>Confirm resistance to the formulation's solvents, since solvent absorption can soften or swell polyolefin tubing</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Polypropylene</td><td>Stiffer than low-density polyethylene and widely used for dip tubes</td><td>Stress-cracking-resistant grades matter where the product is prone to inducing that failure</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Compatibility is confirmed by storage testing of the complete assembly, meaning can, valve, gasket, dip tube and formulation together, and not from material data sheets alone. It is the same principle behind the elastomer testing covered in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-valve-gasket-explained/" target="_top">valve gasket compatibility guide</a>, and it matters most for solvent-rich products that sit in contact with the tube for months.</p>
      </section>

