      <section id="section-5">
        <h2>Testing an Aerosol Dip Tube Design Before Scale-Up</h2>
        <p>A dip tube specification is only proven when filled cans behave the same way from first spray to last. Testing therefore follows the product through its whole life instead of checking a single spray. The core measures are delivery rate, residual product at end of life and spray quality across the can's contents. ASTM D3069, which ASTM now classes as a <a href="https://store.astm.org/d3069-94r05.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">historical standard for delivery rate of aerosol products</a>, states that delivery-rate tests help evaluate one aspect of valve performance and are a prerequisite to storage testing.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Fill representative cans</strong> with the final formulation and propellant, using production-grade dip tubes, valves and cans</li>
          <li><strong>Measure delivery rate</strong> at the start, middle and end of the can's life to confirm that the draw stays steady</li>
          <li><strong>Spray to exhaustion</strong> and weigh the residual product to quantify depletion</li>
          <li><strong>Inspect spray quality</strong> across the can's life, noting any gassy or spitting discharge near the end</li>
          <li><strong>Store the filled cans</strong> and repeat the tests, so swelling, softening or stress cracking of the tube is caught before launch</li>
          <li><strong>Repeat on production-line cans,</strong> because tube length and seating differ between hand-built laboratory cans and line-filled units</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Results from this testing belong in the technical file that supports scale-up, alongside the process controls described in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/from-lab-to-commercial-launch/" target="_top">From Lab to Factory guide</a>. Industry bodies such as <a href="https://bama.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BAMA</a> publish technical guidance for aerosol manufacturers that can help frame acceptance criteria for delivery rate and residual product.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="section-6">
        <h2>Troubleshooting Dip Tube Problems and Design Fixes</h2>
        <p>Dip tube problems show up as a small set of symptoms, and each one points to a specific design variable to check first. Working from symptom to variable shortens troubleshooting and avoids reformulating a product whose real fault is mechanical. The table below maps the most common symptoms to the first variable worth examining.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>First design variable to check</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Product left in a can that has stopped spraying</td><td>Tube length against container height, and the end cut</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Spray turns gassy or spits late in the can's life</td><td>Presence and sizing of a vapor tap, and the tube bore</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Spray rate lower than target</td><td>Tube bore against the body and stem orifices; reducing the bore lowers spray rate</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Tube softens, swells or cracks in storage</td><td>Tube material grade against the formulation, including stress-cracking-resistant grades</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Weak or no draw on the first actuations</td><td>Tube fit and seating at the valve body connection</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>When a product genuinely has to spray from any angle, the fix is architectural and not a longer tube. A bag-on-valve system removes the dip tube altogether, and the trade-offs against a conventional dip-tube can are covered in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/bag-on-valve-technology-design-and-applications/" target="_top">bag-on-valve technology guide</a>. For a conventional can, the design discipline is the one running through this guide: specify length from real geometry, balance bore against the valve path, choose material from storage data, and prove the result on production cans.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What does an aerosol dip tube do?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">The dip tube is the plastic tube that runs from the valve to the bottom of the can. When the valve opens, propellant pressure pushes liquid product up the tube to the valve, so the tube determines where liquid is drawn from and how freely it flows.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How long should an aerosol dip tube be?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">A dip tube should reach as close to the base of the can as the container allows without seating flat against it, matched to the internal can height and the installed valve height. An angled end cut keeps an entry path for liquid open, and the final length is confirmed with a depletion test on production cans.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why does an aerosol spray gassy or spit near the end of the can?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Near the end of the can the liquid level falls to the tube inlet and the tube starts drawing propellant vapor along with the last of the liquid. A vapor tap hole in the tube lets vapor mix with the liquid before it reaches the valve, which substantially re-establishes the spray characteristics late in the can&#x27;s life.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What materials are aerosol dip tubes made from?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Dip tubes are commonly extruded from polyethylene in LDPE, MDPE or HDPE grades, or from polypropylene. The choice is driven by compatibility with the formulation, and stress-cracking-resistant grades matter where the product is prone to causing that failure.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Does dip tube diameter affect spray rate?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Yes. Reducing the internal diameter of the dip tube, like reducing the body or stem orifice, generally lowers the spray rate and particle size and can increase the amount of product retained in the can. The tube bore therefore has to be balanced against the rest of the valve flow path.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Do all aerosol cans use a dip tube?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">No. Bag-on-valve systems hold the product in a sealed laminated pouch separated from the propellant, so they do not use a dip tube and can dispense in any orientation. Conventional upright aerosols rely on a dip tube to reach the liquid at the base of the can.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How is dip tube performance tested?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Dip tube performance is tested by measuring delivery rate at the start, middle and end of the can&#x27;s life, spraying to exhaustion to weigh residual product, and inspecting spray quality throughout. The tests are repeated after storage and on production-line cans to catch tube swelling and variation in tube length and seating.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

